    "body": "One thing worth considering in the design: `walk()` should also expose the folder path for each entry, not just the handle itself.\n\nWhen uploading millions of files, you need to know which folder each file belongs to in order to reconstruct the directory structure on the server side. With the current `entries()` API you lose that context as soon as you go recursive.\n\nSomething like yielding an object with both the handle and its relative path would solve this:\n\n```js\nfor await (const { handle, path } of dirHandle.walk()) {\n  // path: [\"recordings\", \"2024\", \"fieldwork\"] \n  await upload(handle, path);\n}\n```\n\nOr alternatively, keeping it simple and yielding just the handle but with a readable `path` property on it. Either way, without folder context the feature is only half useful for real upload/sync use cases.",
